<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Maynila</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1571 CE (approx.) · capital Maynila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Muslim Tagalog kingdom on the south bank of the Pasig River, closely tied to Brunei, whose capture in 1571 gave Spain its Philippine capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Maynila grew on the southern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasig-river">Pasig River</a> mouth, across from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tondo">Tondo</a>. Tradition holds that around 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolkiah">Sultan Bolkiah</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei</a> conquered it and set up a ruling family allied to Brunei, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-salalila">Rajah Salalila</a>. By the 16th century its elite was Muslim and spoke of the Bruneian court as kin.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Maynila was a fortified port with a palisade and bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a>, trading with Chinese, Bornean and Japanese merchants. Power was shared between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-matanda">Rajah Matanda</a> and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-sulayman">Rajah Sulayman</a>, who led a nobility of datus over freemen and dependants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1570 a Spanish force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-de-goiti">Martin de Goiti</a> burned the town after a clash with Sulayman. In 1571 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel Lopez de Legazpi</a> occupied the site and founded Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>. A coalition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarik-sulayman">Tarik Sulayman</a> of Macabebe, often confused with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-sulayman">Rajah Sulayman</a>, was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bangkusay-channel">Bangkusay</a> that June, and Manila became the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-maynila.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Maynila" width="320" height="349"><figcaption>Image: Delirium333 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Maynila_(1570).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1571 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maynila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rajah Salalila, Rajah Matanda, Rajah Sulayman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Tagalog indigenous religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Tagalog, Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maynila, Rajahnate of Maynila, Seludong</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1536&amp;focus=kingdom-of-maynila" aria-label="Kingdom of Maynila on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8057 1767 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Maynila lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8178 2269h0M8309 1890h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8377 2023h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8377 2023h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Philippines. <a href="/atlas?year=1536&amp;focus=kingdom-of-maynila">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

